\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Surface velocity profile (top row of each block) and interior velocity field (bottom row; arrows coloured by speed magnitude, via Equation~\ref {eq:halfspace_kernel}) for the viscous STEP response (top two rows) and the viscous CSS (bottom two rows), at six illustrative recurrence times $\tau $. Both rows are normalised by the same reference velocity $V$ (the plate rate implied by the CSS cycle period), so arrow colour is directly comparable between STEP and CSS; arrow length is scaled independently within each block for visual clarity. The dashed vertical line in each surface-profile panel marks the diffusion front $L=a\tau $. The single grid point directly above the dike is omitted from the quiver because the near-source field there is disproportionately large relative to the rest of the domain.}}{9}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:viscous_halfspace}{{4}{9}{Surface velocity profile (top row of each block) and interior velocity field (bottom row; arrows coloured by speed magnitude, via Equation~\ref {eq:halfspace_kernel}) for the viscous STEP response (top two rows) and the viscous CSS (bottom two rows), at six illustrative recurrence times $\tau $. Both rows are normalised by the same reference velocity $V$ (the plate rate implied by the CSS cycle period), so arrow colour is directly comparable between STEP and CSS; arrow length is scaled independently within each block for visual clarity. The dashed vertical line in each surface-profile panel marks the diffusion front $L=a\tau $. The single grid point directly above the dike is omitted from the quiver because the near-source field there is disproportionately large relative to the rest of the domain}{figure.4}{}}
