\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Maxwell visco-elastic near-field velocity, at the snapshot time and for the Maxwell length $\ell _M = a\tau _R$ set by the shared-modulus-closure relaxation time of Equation~(\ref {eq:tau_R_shared}) at the Natron best-fit $a$ -- not an independently assumed value, but the one already singled out by that closure for this $a$. Distances are normalised by $\ell _M$, velocities by $b/\tau _R$ evaluated at that same closure value. Grey shading marks the elastic-rebound zone ($x < \ell _M$). Seven values of $\tau _R$, spanning one tenth to ten times the closure value, plus the purely viscous limit (black), which peaks at $x = \ell _M$ (dashed line). Each Maxwell curve flattens into the $x$-independent near-field plateau of Equation~(\ref {eq:maxwell_plateau}) (dotted, same colour), crosses the viscous Lorentzian at the marked point $x_c(\tau _R)$, and converges onto it in the far field. The plateau height is largest when $\tau _R$ matches the closure value and decreases for $\tau _R$ away from it in either direction, while $x_c$ decreases monotonically as $\tau _R$ increases.}}{9}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:maxwell_regimes}{{5}{9}{Maxwell visco-elastic near-field velocity, at the snapshot time and for the Maxwell length $\ell _M = a\tau _R$ set by the shared-modulus-closure relaxation time of Equation~(\ref {eq:tau_R_shared}) at the Natron best-fit $a$ -- not an independently assumed value, but the one already singled out by that closure for this $a$. Distances are normalised by $\ell _M$, velocities by $b/\tau _R$ evaluated at that same closure value. Grey shading marks the elastic-rebound zone ($x < \ell _M$). Seven values of $\tau _R$, spanning one tenth to ten times the closure value, plus the purely viscous limit (black), which peaks at $x = \ell _M$ (dashed line). Each Maxwell curve flattens into the $x$-independent near-field plateau of Equation~(\ref {eq:maxwell_plateau}) (dotted, same colour), crosses the viscous Lorentzian at the marked point $x_c(\tau _R)$, and converges onto it in the far field. The plateau height is largest when $\tau _R$ matches the closure value and decreases for $\tau _R$ away from it in either direction, while $x_c$ decreases monotonically as $\tau _R$ increases}{figure.5}{}}
